\details{
This function takes a GTF file as input, and creates
a \code{GenomicRanges} object that represents genomic regions.
By default, the resulting R object is saved in a file
with file extension \code{".genome_regions.RData"}, so that
the data can be loaded rapidly during subsequent analyses.

The main components of genomic regions defined by this function:
\itemize{
\item \code{"Promoters"}: defined with \code{upstream_promoter} distance upstream
of each gene transcript start site, and \code{downstream_promoter}
distance downstream each gene transcript start site. Note that these
distances are stranded, which means that negative-strand features
\code{strand="-"} will have these distances applied in the opposite
direction.
\item \code{"TTS"}: defined as \code{"Transcript Termination Site"} for each
gene transcript. A region around the TTS is defined by applying
\code{upstream_tts} and \code{downstream_tts} distances, in the same manner
as upstream and downstream distances are applied to \code{"Promoters"}.
\item \code{"Exons"}: defined by gene transcript exons, reduced into
one set of exons for each gene.
\item \code{"Introns"}: defined for each gene, using gene transcript ranges
(from the transcript start site through the transcript termination site)
then subtracting exons. Introns are therefore defined as regions
of a gene locus which are not annotated as exons in any associated
gene transcripts for that gene locus.
}

\strong{Note}: When \code{upstream_tts==0} and \code{downstream_tts==0} the TTS
region is not included in the genome regions, nor any downstream
annotations.

Any region of the genome which is not annotated as a Promoter, TTS,
exon, or intron, is referred to as \code{"extragenic"}, although extragenic
regions are not defined by this function directly. Note that we favor
the term \code{"extragenic"} over \code{"intergenic"} because: the latter term
may be confused with \code{"intragenic"}; and may imply a region that
must be between two existing genes, which is not the case.

In more detail: The arguments \code{geneAttrNames}, \code{txAttrNames}, and
\code{geneFeatureType} are used by \code{splicejam::makeTx2geneFromGtf()} to
create a \code{data.frame} which relates \code{transcript_id} to each \code{gene_name}
by default. If the GTF file uses different attributes, adjust
\code{geneAttrNames} and \code{txAttrNames} to match the GTF file.
\subsection{Comments on compatible files}{

The function itself is capable of reading GTF or GFF3 formatted files,
by calling \code{txdbmaker::makeTxDbFromGFF()} to do that heavy work.
However \code{splicejam::makeTx2geneFromGtf()} is required to import
additional gene and transcript annotations.

We found it is helpful for column 9 of the GTF or GFF3 file to contain
attributes:
\itemize{
\item gene_id: a unique gene identifier
\item gene_name: a unique gene symbol associated with each gene_id
\item transcript_id: one or more transcript identifiers associated with
a gene_id
}
}

\subsection{Issues with 'Name'}{

We found it problematic when the GTF or GFF3 file contains \code{"Name"},
specifically in cases where the same value is associated with multiple
rows in the file. For example, a row with type "gene", \code{Name=APOE;}, then
on the next row for type "transcript", also assigns \code{Name=APOE;}.
Apparently this input causes \code{txdbmaker::makeTxDbFromGFF()} to assign
the Name value as the primary identifier for both the gene and the
transcript, and therefore interferes with the \code{tx2gene} association
of \code{gene_id} to \code{gene_name}, and \code{gene_id} to \code{transcript_id}.

This type of formatting is present in the human telomere-to-telomere
(T2T) v2.0 GTF file (as of November 2022).

In this case, we found it successful to remove the \code{Name} entries
from the GTF or GFF3 file, then call \code{genomic_regions_from_gtf()},
potentially with \code{force_refresh=TRUE} to ensure the file is re-processed.
While inconvenient, in principle the GTF or GFF3 file is incorrect by
using the same \code{Name} value for multiple features. We did not want
to modify this function (yet) to accommodate an incorrect GTF or GFF3
input file.

All that said, we are incredibly grateful and happy to use
the human T2T v2.0 GTF gene annotations, which contain very high
quality gene annotations.
}
